[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Styling, Flexbox Layout Engine (Yoga), and Platform-Specific Styling in React Native"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"Styling in React Native bridges the gap between web development concepts and native mobile UI performance. React Native uses JavaScript objects for styles and leverages Yoga, an open-source, cross-platform layout engine implementing Flexbox, to handle UI positioning. In this tutorial, you will learn how to structure styles efficiently, master the Yoga Flexbox engine, and write platform-specific code for iOS and Android."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Styling Basics in React Native"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unlike CSS files on the web, React Native uses StyleSheet.create() to encapsulate styles. Property names generally follow camelCase conventions (e.g., backgroundColor instead of background-color)."},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"import React from 'react';
import { StyleSheet, Text, View } from 'react-native';

export default function App() {
  return (
    <View style={styles.container}>
      <Text style={styles.text}>Hello, React Native Styling!</Text>
    </View>
  );
}

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: {
    flex: 1,
    backgroundColor: '#f5fcff',
    justifyContent: 'center',
    alignItems: 'center',
  },
  text: {
    fontSize: 20,
    fontWeight: 'bold',
    color: '#333333',
  },
});"},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"2. The Flexbox Layout Engine (Yoga)"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"React Native layout is built entirely on Flexbox using Facebook's Yoga engine. However, there are a few key differences compared to CSS Flexbox:"},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"flexDirection defaults to 'column' instead of 'row'."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The flex property accepts a single number (e.g., flex: 1) rather than the CSS shorthand flex grow/shrink/basis values."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Percentage dimensions are supported for properties like width and height."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"const flexStyles = StyleSheet.create({
  rowContainer: {
    flex: 1,
    flexDirection: 'row',
    justifyContent: 'space-around',
    alignItems: 'center',
  },
  box: {
    width: 80,
    height: 80,
    backgroundColor: '#ff5722',
    borderRadius: 8,
  },
});"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Platform-Specific Styling"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Different platforms often require unique UI patterns, such as shadows on iOS versus elevation on Android. React Native provides the Platform API and platform-specific file extensions to handle these variations cleanly."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"import { Platform, StyleSheet } from 'react-native';

const platformStyles = StyleSheet.create({
  card: {
    backgroundColor: '#ffffff',
    padding: 16,
    margin: 12,
    borderRadius: 8,
    ...Platform.select({
      ios: {
        shadowColor: '#000',
        shadowOffset: { width: 0, height: 2 },
        shadowOpacity: 0.2,
        shadowRadius: 4,
      },
      android: {
        elevation: 4,
      },
    }),
  },
});"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"By combining StyleSheet optimizations, the Yoga Flexbox engine, and Platform-specific conditional styles, you can build responsive, highly performant user interfaces that look native on both iOS and Android platforms."}]
